inference_trace:
Sort the array so every possible child value comes before its parent. Let `dp[i]` be the number of trees whose root is `arr[i]`.

- A single node is always a valid tree, so `dp[i]` starts at 1.
- For each smaller value `arr[j]` that divides `arr[i]`, check whether the quotient `arr[i] / arr[j]` is also in the array.
- If it is (at index `k`), add `dp[j] * dp[k]` to `dp[i]`.
- Ordered pairs count separately, so `(2, 5)` and `(5, 2)` are two different trees. A pair like `(2, 2)` is counted once, which is correct.

Implementation details:

- A dictionary maps each value to its index, so the quotient lookup is O(1).
- All arithmetic is done in `long` and reduced modulo 1e9+7.
- The answer is the sum of all `dp[i]`.
- Total time is O(n²) after sorting, which is fine for n ≤ 1000.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int NumFactoredBinaryTrees(int[] arr) {
        const long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;
        Array.Sort(arr);
        int n = arr.Length;
        var idx = new Dictionary<int, int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) idx[arr[i]] = i;
        long[] dp = new long[n];
        long total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            dp[i] = 1;
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (arr[i] % arr[j] == 0) {
                    int q = arr[i] / arr[j];
                    if (idx.TryGetValue(q, out int k)) {
                        dp[i] = (dp[i] + dp[j] * dp[k]) % MOD;
                    }
                }
            }
            total = (total + dp[i]) % MOD;
        }
        return (int)total;
    }
}
```

