Take the first two points as the reference line. Every other point must be collinear with them, which we can check with a cross product instead of comparing slopes (this avoids division and vertical-line special cases).

1. Let the first point be `(x0, y0)` and the direction to the second point be `(dx, dy) = (x1 - x0, y1 - y0)`.
2. For each point `(x, y)`, it lies on the line exactly when `dx * (y - y0) - dy * (x - x0) == 0`.
3. Return `false` as soon as any point fails this test; otherwise return `true`.

Overflow is not a concern: each difference is at most 2·10^4 in absolute value, so each product is at most 4·10^8, which fits in an `int`. Using `long` anyway costs nothing.

With only two points, the loop finds nothing to reject and returns `true`, which is correct.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool CheckStraightLine(int[][] coordinates) {
        long x0 = coordinates[0][0], y0 = coordinates[0][1];
        long dx = coordinates[1][0] - x0, dy = coordinates[1][1] - y0;
        for (int i = 2; i < coordinates.Length; i++) {
            long x = coordinates[i][0] - x0, y = coordinates[i][1] - y0;
            if (dx * y - dy * x != 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

