inference_trace:
Walk through both arrays at the same time with two pointers each: one pointer for which word we are in, and one for the character position inside that word. This avoids building the full concatenated strings.

1. At each step, compare the current characters of the two sides. If they differ, return `false`.
2. Advance the character pointer on each side. When it reaches the end of a word, move to the next word and reset the character pointer to 0.
3. The loop stops when either side runs out of words. Return `true` only if both sides ran out at the same time.

Every word has length at least 1, so moving to the next word always lands on a valid character.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool ArrayStringsAreEqual(string[] word1, string[] word2) {
        int i = 0, a = 0, j = 0, b = 0;
        while (i < word1.Length && j < word2.Length) {
            if (word1[i][a] != word2[j][b]) return false;
            a++; b++;
            if (a == word1[i].Length) { i++; a = 0; }
            if (b == word2[j].Length) { j++; b = 0; }
        }
        return i == word1.Length && j == word2.Length;
    }
}
```

