The approach is to convert each word into its numerical value, then compare.

- For each letter, the digit is `c - 'a'`.
- Build the number by multiplying the running value by 10 and adding the digit.
- Words have at most 8 letters, so each value is at most 99,999,999, which fits in an `int`.
- The sum of two such values is at most about 2×10^8, which is still below `int.MaxValue` (about 2.1×10^9).
- The example inputs show trailing spaces (like `"acb "`). Those are formatting artifacts, but to be safe the code skips any character outside `'a'` to `'j'`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool IsSumEqual(string firstWord, string secondWord, string targetWord) {
        return Val(firstWord) + Val(secondWord) == Val(targetWord);
    }

    private long Val(string s) {
        long v = 0;
        foreach (char c in s) {
            if (c < 'a' || c > 'j') continue;
            v = v * 10 + (c - 'a');
        }
        return v;
    }
}
```

