inference_trace:
The string lengths are at most 10, so f(s) is always between 1 and 10. That makes counting easy.

1. For each word, compute f(word): find its smallest character and count how often it appears. Tally these values in a count array indexed 1..10.
2. Build a suffix count, where `suffix[k]` is the number of words with f > k.
3. For each query, compute f(query) and return `suffix[f(query)]`.

When computing f, I skip any character outside 'a'–'z', since the examples show trailing spaces in the strings.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    private int F(string s) {
        char mn = '{';
        int cnt = 0;
        foreach (char c in s) {
            if (c < 'a' || c > 'z') continue;
            if (c < mn) { mn = c; cnt = 1; }
            else if (c == mn) cnt++;
        }
        return cnt;
    }
    public int[] NumSmallerByFrequency(string[] queries, string[] words) {
        int[] cnt = new int[13];
        foreach (var w in words) cnt[F(w)]++;
        int[] suf = new int[13];
        for (int k = 11; k >= 0; k--) suf[k] = suf[k + 1] + cnt[k + 1];
        int[] res = new int[queries.Length];
        for (int i = 0; i < queries.Length; i++) {
            int f = F(queries[i]);
            res[i] = f >= 12 ? 0 : suf[f];
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

