inference_trace:
The maximum possible OR is simply the OR of every element, since adding elements can never clear a bit. So the task reduces to counting subsets whose OR equals that total.

With at most 16 elements there are at most 2^16 = 65,536 subsets, so we can enumerate them all:

1. Compute `target` as the OR of all elements in `nums`.
2. Build an array `dp` of size 2^n, where `dp[mask]` holds the OR of the elements selected by `mask`.
3. Fill it incrementally:
   - Let `low = mask & -mask`, the lowest set bit of `mask`.
   - Let `idx` be the position of that bit.
   - Then `dp[mask] = dp[mask ^ low] | nums[idx]`.
4. For every non-empty mask, add one to the count when `dp[mask] == target`.

The total work is O(2^n · n) in the worst case, which is easily fast enough for n ≤ 16.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int CountMaxOrSubsets(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        int target = 0;
        foreach (int x in nums) target |= x;
        int total = 1 << n;
        int[] dp = new int[total];
        int count = 0;
        for (int mask = 1; mask < total; mask++) {
            int low = mask & -mask;
            int idx = 0;
            while ((1 << idx) != low) idx++;
            dp[mask] = dp[mask ^ low] | nums[idx];
            if (dp[mask] == target) count++;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

