Since `word.length` is at most 100, a brute-force O(n²) scan is fast enough.

1. For each start index `i`, extend the substring to the right one character at a time.
2. Stop extending as soon as a non-vowel appears, because no longer substring from `i` can be a vowel substring.
3. While extending, keep a 5-bit mask recording which vowels have been seen.
4. Each time the mask equals 31 (all five bits set), the current substring contains every vowel, so add one to the count.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int CountVowelSubstrings(string word) {
        int n = word.Length, count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int mask = 0;
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                int b;
                switch (word[j]) {
                    case 'a': b = 1; break;
                    case 'e': b = 2; break;
                    case 'i': b = 4; break;
                    case 'o': b = 8; break;
                    case 'u': b = 16; break;
                    default: b = 0; break;
                }
                if (b == 0) break;
                mask |= b;
                if (mask == 31) count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

