Use lazy increments so every operation runs in O(1).

- Keep the stack in a fixed array `stk` of size `maxSize`, with `top` counting the current elements.
- Keep a parallel array `inc`, where `inc[i]` is a pending increment that applies to every element at index `i` and below.
- **Push(x):** if the stack is not full, store `x` at `stk[top]` and clear `inc[top]`, then advance `top`.
- **Increment(k, val):** let `i = min(k, top) - 1`. If `i >= 0`, add `val` to `inc[i]`. This marks the bottom `i + 1` elements without touching them.
- **Pop():** if the stack is empty, return -1. Otherwise take index `i = top - 1` and return `stk[i] + inc[i]`. Before returning, pass `inc[i]` down to `inc[i - 1]` (when `i > 0`), so the elements below still receive it.

```csharp
public class CustomStack {
    private int[] stk;
    private int[] inc;
    private int top;

    public CustomStack(int maxSize) {
        stk = new int[maxSize];
        inc = new int[maxSize];
        top = 0;
    }

    public void Push(int x) {
        if (top < stk.Length) {
            stk[top] = x;
            inc[top] = 0;
            top++;
        }
    }

    public int Pop() {
        if (top == 0) return -1;
        int i = top - 1;
        int res = stk[i] + inc[i];
        if (i > 0) inc[i - 1] += inc[i];
        inc[i] = 0;
        top--;
        return res;
    }

    public void Increment(int k, int val) {
        int i = Math.Min(k, top) - 1;
        if (i >= 0) inc[i] += val;
    }
}
```

