Two strings are close exactly when both of these hold:

1. **Same set of characters.** Neither operation can introduce a character that isn't already present, or remove one that is. So every letter that appears in one string must appear in the other.
2. **Same multiset of frequencies.** Operation 1 (swap) only reorders characters, so counts are unchanged. Operation 2 swaps the counts of two existing letters. Together they can assign the existing counts to the existing letters in any arrangement, but the collection of count values never changes.

The algorithm:

- If the lengths differ, return `false`.
- Count each letter's occurrences in both strings.
- If any letter is present in one string but absent in the other, return `false`.
- Sort both count arrays and compare them element by element.

This runs in O(n) time, since sorting 26 values is constant work.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool CloseStrings(string word1, string word2) {
        if (word1.Length != word2.Length) return false;
        int[] a = new int[26], b = new int[26];
        foreach (char c in word1) a[c - 'a']++;
        foreach (char c in word2) b[c - 'a']++;
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if ((a[i] == 0) != (b[i] == 0)) return false;
        }
        System.Array.Sort(a);
        System.Array.Sort(b);
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

