Use a greedy two-pointer approach. Keep `lo = 0` and `hi = n`.

- On `'I'`, place `lo` and increment it. Every number still unused is larger than `lo`, so whatever comes next will be greater.
- On `'D'`, place `hi` and decrement it. Every number still unused is smaller than `hi`, so whatever comes next will be smaller.
- After processing all `n` characters, `lo == hi`, and that last remaining number fills the final position.

This runs in O(n) time.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int[] DiStringMatch(string s) {
        int n = s.Length;
        int[] res = new int[n + 1];
        int lo = 0, hi = n;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (s[i] == 'I') res[i] = lo++;
            else res[i] = hi--;
        }
        res[n] = lo;
        return res;
    }
}
```

