For each index `j` where `nums[j] == key`, every index in the window `[j - k, j + k]` (clamped to the array bounds) is a k-distant index.

1. Keep a pointer `next` for the smallest index not yet added to the result.
2. Scan `j` from left to right. When `nums[j] == key`:
   - Start adding at `max(next, j - k)`.
   - Stop at `min(n - 1, j + k)`.
   - Set `next` to one past the last index added.
3. Because `next` only moves forward, each index is added at most once. The result therefore comes out sorted with no duplicates.

This runs in O(n) time.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public IList<int> FindKDistantIndices(int[] nums, int key, int k) {
        var res = new List<int>();
        int n = nums.Length;
        int next = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (nums[j] == key) {
                int start = Math.Max(next, j - k);
                int end = Math.Min(n - 1, j + k);
                for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) res.Add(i);
                next = Math.Max(next, end + 1);
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

