Approach: put the values of `nums` into a HashSet for O(1) lookups, then walk the linked list once.

- A connected component is a maximal run of consecutive list nodes whose values are all in the set.
- Each component has exactly one last node. That node is in the set, and either it is the tail of the list or its next node is not in the set.
- So the answer is the number of nodes that satisfy both conditions: their value is in the set, and the next node is null or not in the set.

This runs in O(n + m) time and O(m) extra space, where n is the list length and m is the size of `nums`.

```csharp
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * public class ListNode {
 *     public int val;
 *     public ListNode next;
 *     public ListNode(int val=0, ListNode next=null) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.next = next;
 *     }
 * }
 */
public class Solution {
    public int NumComponents(ListNode head, int[] nums) {
        var set = new HashSet<int>(nums);
        int count = 0;
        ListNode cur = head;
        while (cur != null) {
            if (set.Contains(cur.val) && (cur.next == null || !set.Contains(cur.next.val))) {
                count++;
            }
            cur = cur.next;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

