Use patience sorting with binary search, which runs in O(n log n).

Keep an array `tails`, where `tails[i]` is the smallest possible tail value of any strictly increasing subsequence of length `i + 1` seen so far. Its first `len` entries are always sorted.

For each number `x`:
1. Binary search for the first position in `tails[0..len)` whose value is `>= x`. This is a lower bound, which keeps the subsequence strictly increasing, so equal values do not extend it.
2. Write `x` into that position.
3. If the position equals `len`, `x` extends the longest subsequence, so increment `len`.

The answer is `len`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int LengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int[] tails = new int[nums.Length];
        int len = 0;
        foreach (int x in nums) {
            int lo = 0, hi = len;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
                if (tails[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1;
                else hi = mid;
            }
            tails[lo] = x;
            if (lo == len) len++;
        }
        return len;
    }
}
```

