inference_trace:
Approach: dynamic programming over words processed from shortest to longest.

1. Sort the words by length, so every possible predecessor of a word is handled before the word itself.
2. Keep a dictionary `dp` mapping each word to the length of the longest chain that ends with it.
3. For each word, try deleting each character in turn. If the resulting string is already in `dp`, the chain ending at that string can be extended by one.
4. Store the best value for the current word (at least 1) and track the overall maximum.

Duplicate words cause no problem: a word's deletions are always one character shorter, so it never matches itself.

Complexity: with n ≤ 1000 words of length L ≤ 16, there are n·L deletions, each costing O(L) to build and hash. That is O(n·L²) time, which is tiny.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution {
    public int LongestStrChain(string[] words) {
        var sorted = words.OrderBy(w => w.Length).ToArray();
        var dp = new Dictionary<string, int>();
        int best = 0;
        foreach (var w in sorted) {
            int cur = 1;
            for (int i = 0; i < w.Length; i++) {
                string p = w.Remove(i, 1);
                if (dp.TryGetValue(p, out int v) && v + 1 > cur) cur = v + 1;
            }
            if (!dp.TryGetValue(w, out int ex) || ex < cur) dp[w] = cur;
            if (cur > best) best = cur;
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

