inference_trace:
For each point, compute the slope to every later point and count how many share the same slope.

1. Represent a slope by `(dx, dy)` reduced by their gcd.
2. Normalize the sign so the same direction always gets the same key: make `dx > 0`, or if `dx == 0`, make `dy > 0`.
3. Points are unique, so `dx` and `dy` are never both zero and no duplicate handling is needed.
4. Encode the pair as a single `long` key, `dx * 100000 + dy`. The coordinates lie in `[-10^4, 10^4]`, so differences lie in `[-2·10^4, 2·10^4]` and the key is unique.
5. For each anchor point, the best line through it has (largest slope count + 1) points. The answer is the maximum over all anchors, which is at least 1.

This is O(n²) pairs, about 90k for n = 300, which is fast enough.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int MaxPoints(int[][] points) {
        int n = points.Length;
        int best = 1;
        var map = new Dictionary<long, int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            map.Clear();
            int localMax = 0;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                int dx = points[j][0] - points[i][0];
                int dy = points[j][1] - points[i][1];
                int g = Gcd(Math.Abs(dx), Math.Abs(dy));
                dx /= g; dy /= g;
                if (dx < 0 || (dx == 0 && dy < 0)) { dx = -dx; dy = -dy; }
                long key = (long)dx * 100000L + dy;
                map.TryGetValue(key, out int c);
                c++;
                map[key] = c;
                if (c > localMax) localMax = c;
            }
            if (localMax + 1 > best) best = localMax + 1;
        }
        return best;
    }

    private int Gcd(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) { int t = a % b; a = b; b = t; }
        return a;
    }
}
```

