inference_trace:
Use a bitmask DP over "slot positions". Treat each slot as two separate positions, giving 2·numSlots ≤ 18 positions in total.

- Let `dp[mask]` be the best AND sum after placing the first `popcount(mask)` numbers into the positions marked in `mask`.
- Position `j` belongs to slot `j/2 + 1`.
- For each mask with `i = popcount(mask) < n`, place `nums[i]` into every free position `j`:
  `dp[mask | (1<<j)] = max(dp[mask | (1<<j)], dp[mask] + (nums[i] & (j/2 + 1)))`.
- Unreachable states start at -1 and are skipped.
- The answer is the maximum `dp` value over all masks whose popcount equals `n`.

Complexity: 2^18 ≈ 262k masks × 18 transitions ≈ 4.7M operations, which is fine.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MaximumANDSum(int[] nums, int numSlots) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        int m = 2 * numSlots;
        int size = 1 << m;
        int[] dp = new int[size];
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) dp[i] = -1;
        dp[0] = 0;
        int best = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < size; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;
            int i = System.Numerics.BitOperations.PopCount((uint)mask);
            if (i == n) {
                if (dp[mask] > best) best = dp[mask];
                continue;
            }
            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << j)) != 0) continue;
                int nm = mask | (1 << j);
                int v = dp[mask] + (nums[i] & (j / 2 + 1));
                if (v > dp[nm]) dp[nm] = v;
            }
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

