inference_trace:
If the string has no `'0'`, it is already all ones, so return it unchanged.

Otherwise:
- Let `first` be the index of the first `'0'`.
- Let `zeros` be the total number of `'0'` characters.
- Operation 2 ("10" → "01") lets us slide every zero leftward until all zeros sit in one block starting at `first`. The ones before `first` stay in place.
- Operation 1 ("00" → "10") then turns every zero in that block into a one except the last.
- So exactly one `'0'` remains, at position `first + zeros - 1`, and every other position is `'1'`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public string MaximumBinaryString(string binary) {
        int first = binary.IndexOf('0');
        if (first < 0) return binary;
        int zeros = 0;
        foreach (char c in binary) if (c == '0') zeros++;
        char[] res = new char[binary.Length];
        for (int i = 0; i < res.Length; i++) res[i] = '1';
        res[first + zeros - 1] = '0';
        return new string(res);
    }
}
```

