inference_trace:
This is an assignment problem with at most 8 students and 8 mentors, so a bitmask DP over the set of used mentors is small enough.

1. **Precompute scores.** For every student `i` and mentor `j`, store `score[i][j]`, the number of questions where their answers match.
2. **Define the DP.** `dp[mask]` is the best total score after assigning students `0 .. popcount(mask) - 1`, where the set bits of `mask` are the mentors already used.
3. **Transition.** From `dp[mask]`, the next student is `i = popcount(mask)`. For each mentor `j` not in `mask`, update `dp[mask | 1 << j]` with `dp[mask] + score[i][j]`.
4. **Answer.** The result is `dp[(1 << m) - 1]`, where every mentor has been used.

**Complexity.** There are `2^m` masks and up to `m` transitions each, so time is O(2^m · m), plus O(m²·n) to precompute scores. With m = 8 that is 256 · 8 = 2048 transitions.

Unreachable states are marked with -1 so they are skipped.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MaxCompatibilitySum(int[][] students, int[][] mentors) {
        int m = students.Length, n = students[0].Length;
        int[,] score = new int[m, m];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                int s = 0;
                for (int k = 0; k < n; k++)
                    if (students[i][k] == mentors[j][k]) s++;
                score[i, j] = s;
            }
        int full = 1 << m;
        int[] dp = new int[full];
        for (int i = 1; i < full; i++) dp[i] = -1;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;
            int i = System.Numerics.BitOperations.PopCount((uint)mask);
            if (i >= m) continue;
            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << j)) != 0) continue;
                int nm = mask | (1 << j);
                int v = dp[mask] + score[i, j];
                if (v > dp[nm]) dp[nm] = v;
            }
        }
        return dp[full - 1];
    }
}
```

