inference_trace:
Track two arrays while scanning the prefix:
- `cnt[x]`: how many times value `x` has appeared so far.
- `freq[f]`: how many distinct values currently have exactly `f` occurrences.
- `maxF`: the highest frequency seen so far.

For a prefix of length `len = i + 1`, removing exactly one element can make all frequencies equal in these cases:

1. **`maxF == 1`**: every value appears once. Removing any one element leaves all values with count 1 (or an empty array).
2. **`freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == len`**: every value has count `maxF` except one extra element. That extra element must be a value with count 1, and we remove it.
3. **`freq[maxF - 1] * (maxF - 1) + maxF == len`** and `freq[maxF] == 1`: exactly one value has count `maxF`, and all others have count `maxF - 1`. Removing one occurrence of the top value equalizes them.
   - When `maxF - 1 == 0`, this reduces to `maxF == len`, meaning there is a single distinct value. That situation is also valid.
4. **`freq[maxF] == 1` and `maxF == len`**: the single-value case, already covered by case 3.

Whenever any condition holds, record `len` as the answer.

**Bounds:** values go up to 1e5, and frequencies can reach `n`, so both arrays are sized `max(n, 1e5) + 2`.

**Check with example 1, len = 7 (`[2,2,1,1,5,3,3]`):** counts are 2:2, 1:2, 5:1, 3:2, so `maxF = 2` and `freq[2] = 3`. Then `3*2 + 1 = 7`, which satisfies case 2.

**Edge case, `[1,1]`, len = 2:** `maxF = 2` and `freq[2] = 1`. Case 3 gives `freq[1] * 1 + 2 = 0 + 2 = 2`, which equals `len`, so the prefix is valid.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MaxEqualFreq(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        int size = System.Math.Max(n, 100000) + 2;
        int[] cnt = new int[size];
        int[] freq = new int[size];
        int maxF = 0, ans = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int x = nums[i];
            if (cnt[x] > 0) freq[cnt[x]]--;
            cnt[x]++;
            freq[cnt[x]]++;
            if (cnt[x] > maxF) maxF = cnt[x];
            int len = i + 1;
            bool ok = false;
            if (maxF == 1) ok = true;
            else if (freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == len) ok = true;
            else if (freq[maxF] == 1 && freq[maxF - 1] * (maxF - 1) + maxF == len) ok = true;
            if (ok) ans = len;
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

