inference_trace:
Binary search on the answer T. Running all `n` computers for T minutes is feasible exactly when the total usable charge, the sum of `min(b, T)` over all batteries, is at least `n * T`. A single battery can contribute at most T minutes, because it can power only one computer at a time.

Bounds:
- The total charge is at most 1e5 × 1e9 = 1e14, so the answer is at most `sum / n`.
- `n * T` stays within about 1e14, which fits in a `long`.

Search:
- Start with `lo = 0`, `hi = sum / n`.
- Take `mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2`.
- If `mid` is feasible, set `lo = mid`; otherwise set `hi = mid - 1`.
- Each check is O(m), so the total cost is O(m log(sum)), which is fine.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public long MaxRunTime(int n, int[] batteries) {
        long sum = 0;
        foreach (var b in batteries) sum += b;
        long lo = 0, hi = sum / n;
        while (lo < hi) {
            long mid = lo + (hi - lo + 1) / 2;
            long total = 0;
            foreach (var b in batteries) total += Math.Min((long)b, mid);
            if (total >= (long)n * mid) lo = mid;
            else hi = mid - 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }
}
```

