inference_trace:
Approach: sort the gardens, choose how many of the fullest ones to complete, and maximize the minimum of the rest.

1. **Cap and sort.** Cap every garden at `target`, since extra flowers beyond `target` never help. Then sort ascending and build prefix sums.

2. **Complete a suffix.** It is always best to complete the gardens that are already closest to `target`, which are the largest ones. So try `j = 0..n` gardens completed from the end.
   - Keep a running cost of completing them.
   - Stop as soon as that cost exceeds `newFlowers`.

3. **All gardens complete.** If `j == n`, the beauty is `n * full`.

4. **Some gardens incomplete.** Otherwise the incomplete gardens are `arr[0..n-j-1]`.
   - If `arr[n-j-1] == target`, that garden is already complete. Skip this `j`; a larger `j` covers it at zero extra cost.
   - Otherwise, with remaining budget `rem`, find the largest `p` in `[1, n-j]` such that raising the first `p` gardens to `arr[p-1]` costs at most `rem`. That cost is `arr[p-1]*p - prefix[p]`, which is monotone in `p`, so binary search works.
   - The achievable minimum is `min(target-1, (rem + prefix[p]) / p)`.
   - The beauty for this `j` is `j*full + minimum*partial`.

5. **Answer.** Take the maximum beauty over all `j`.

Total time is O(n log n). All arithmetic uses `long`, since values reach about 1e10.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public long MaximumBeauty(int[] flowers, long newFlowers, int target, int full, int partial) {
        int n = flowers.Length;
        long[] arr = new long[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = Math.Min(flowers[i], target);
        Array.Sort(arr);
        long[] pre = new long[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + arr[i];
        long best = 0;
        long cost = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
            if (j > 0) cost += target - arr[n - j];
            if (cost > newFlowers) break;
            long rem = newFlowers - cost;
            if (j == n) {
                best = Math.Max(best, (long)n * full);
                break;
            }
            if (arr[n - j - 1] >= target) continue;
            int lo = 1, hi = n - j;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2;
                if (arr[mid - 1] * mid - pre[mid] <= rem) lo = mid; else hi = mid - 1;
            }
            int p = lo;
            long m = Math.Min((long)target - 1, (rem + pre[p]) / p);
            long val = (long)j * full + m * partial;
            if (val > best) best = val;
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

