Use two pointers: `slow` moves one node per step and `fast` moves two. When `fast` reaches the end of the list, `slow` is at the middle.

- **Odd length** (e.g. 5 nodes): `fast` lands on the last node, so `fast.next` is null and the loop stops. `slow` has moved 2 steps and is on node 3, the exact middle.
- **Even length** (e.g. 6 nodes): `fast` steps past the last node and becomes null. `slow` has moved 3 steps and is on node 4, the second middle, as required.

This runs in O(n) time and O(1) extra space.

```csharp
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * public class ListNode {
 *     public int val;
 *     public ListNode next;
 *     public ListNode(int val=0, ListNode next=null) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.next = next;
 *     }
 * }
 */
public class Solution {
    public ListNode MiddleNode(ListNode head) {
        ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
        }
        return slow;
    }
}
```

