Approach: treat every pair of points as two opposite corners of a candidate rectangle, then check whether the other two corners exist.

1. Put every point into a hash set, encoded as the key `x * 40001 + y` in a `long`. Since coordinates are at most 40000, this key is unique per point.
2. For each pair of points `(i, j)` with different x and different y, they could be diagonal corners. The other two corners are `(x1, y2)` and `(x2, y1)`.
3. If both of those points are in the set, compute the area `|x1 - x2| * |y1 - y2|` and keep the minimum.
4. If no rectangle was found, return 0.

With at most 500 points there are about 125,000 pairs, each checked in O(1) average time, so this is O(n²) overall.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int MinAreaRect(int[][] points) {
        var set = new HashSet<long>();
        foreach (var p in points) set.Add((long)p[0] * 40001 + p[1]);
        int best = int.MaxValue;
        int n = points.Length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int x1 = points[i][0], y1 = points[i][1];
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                int x2 = points[j][0], y2 = points[j][1];
                if (x1 == x2 || y1 == y2) continue;
                int area = Math.Abs(x1 - x2) * Math.Abs(y1 - y2);
                if (area >= best) continue;
                if (set.Contains((long)x1 * 40001 + y2) && set.Contains((long)x2 * 40001 + y1)) {
                    best = area;
                }
            }
        }
        return best == int.MaxValue ? 0 : best;
    }
}
```

