DP over time: let dp[t][v] be the minimum cost to stand at city v having used exactly t minutes.

- Start with dp[0][0] = passingFees[0]; every other state is infinity.
- For each t from 1 to maxTime, look at every edge (x, y, w) with w ≤ t:
  - dp[t][x] = min(dp[t][x], dp[t-w][y] + passingFees[x]).
  - dp[t][y] = min(dp[t][y], dp[t-w][x] + passingFees[y]).
- The answer is the minimum of dp[t][n-1] over all t ≤ maxTime, or -1 if it stays infinity.

Complexity is O(maxTime · E), about 1e6 operations. Memory is 1001 × 1000 ints, about 4MB.

Revisiting a city charges its fee again. That is harmless: fees are positive, so a revisit never yields a lower cost and the optimum always uses a simple path.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MinCost(int maxTime, int[][] edges, int[] passingFees) {
        int n = passingFees.Length;
        const int INF = int.MaxValue / 2;
        int[][] dp = new int[maxTime + 1][];
        for (int t = 0; t <= maxTime; t++) {
            dp[t] = new int[n];
            Array.Fill(dp[t], INF);
        }
        dp[0][0] = passingFees[0];
        int ans = INF;
        if (n - 1 == 0) ans = passingFees[0];
        for (int t = 1; t <= maxTime; t++) {
            int[] cur = dp[t];
            foreach (var e in edges) {
                int x = e[0], y = e[1], w = e[2];
                if (w > t) continue;
                int[] prev = dp[t - w];
                if (prev[y] < INF) {
                    int c = prev[y] + passingFees[x];
                    if (c < cur[x]) cur[x] = c;
                }
                if (prev[x] < INF) {
                    int c = prev[x] + passingFees[y];
                    if (c < cur[y]) cur[y] = c;
                }
            }
            if (cur[n - 1] < ans) ans = cur[n - 1];
        }
        return ans >= INF ? -1 : ans;
    }
}
```

