Approach:

- The answer is always at most 2. Some corner land cell of any island can be cut off by removing its two neighbours.
- **Answer 0:** if the number of islands is not exactly 1, the grid is already disconnected.
- **Answer 1:** otherwise, try removing each land cell in turn and count islands again. If any removal gives a count other than 1, one day is enough. This also covers a single land cell, since removing it leaves 0 islands.
- **Answer 2:** if no single removal works.

Cost: there are up to 900 land cells, and each island count is an O(900) BFS. That is about 810k operations, which is fine.

Implementation notes:
- `Count()` runs an iterative BFS over all land cells, using a fresh visited array each call, and returns the number of islands.
- I modify `grid` in place for each trial removal and restore the cell afterwards.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    int m, n;
    int[] dr = {1,-1,0,0};
    int[] dc = {0,0,1,-1};

    int Count(int[][] g) {
        var vis = new bool[m, n];
        int cnt = 0;
        var q = new Queue<int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (g[i][j] == 1 && !vis[i, j]) {
                    cnt++;
                    vis[i, j] = true;
                    q.Enqueue(i * n + j);
                    while (q.Count > 0) {
                        int c = q.Dequeue();
                        int r = c / n, cc = c % n;
                        for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                            int nr = r + dr[d], nc = cc + dc[d];
                            if (nr >= 0 && nr < m && nc >= 0 && nc < n && g[nr][nc] == 1 && !vis[nr, nc]) {
                                vis[nr, nc] = true;
                                q.Enqueue(nr * n + nc);
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        return cnt;
    }

    public int MinDays(int[][] grid) {
        m = grid.Length; n = grid[0].Length;
        if (Count(grid) != 1) return 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    grid[i][j] = 0;
                    int c = Count(grid);
                    grid[i][j] = 1;
                    if (c != 1) return 1;
                }
            }
        return 2;
    }
}
```

