inference_trace:
The approach is a memoized recursion. It relies on one observation: it is only worth eating single oranges to reach the nearest multiple of 2 or 3, and then using the big move.

Define `f(n)` as the minimum number of days to eat `n` oranges.

- Base case: `f(n) = n` when `n <= 1`.
- Otherwise: `f(n) = 1 + min(n % 2 + f(n / 2), n % 3 + f(n / 3))`.

In the first option, `n % 2` single-orange days bring the count to a multiple of 2, and one day halves it. The second option does the same with 3, leaving a third.

Memoizing with a dictionary keeps the number of distinct states at roughly O(log² n), so `n` up to 2·10⁹ is fast.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    private Dictionary<int,int> memo = new Dictionary<int,int>();
    public int MinDays(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        if (memo.TryGetValue(n, out int v)) return v;
        int res = 1 + Math.Min(n % 2 + MinDays(n / 2), n % 3 + MinDays(n / 3));
        memo[n] = res;
        return res;
    }
}
```

