inference_trace:
This uses binary search on the day.

1. **Impossible case:** if `m * k` is greater than `n`, there are not enough flowers, so return -1. Compute `m * k` as a `long`, since it can reach 10^11.
2. **Otherwise an answer exists:** on the largest bloom day every flower has bloomed. That gives `n / k` bouquets, which is at least `m`.
3. **Monotonic check:** if `m` bouquets can be made by day `d`, they can be made on any later day. So we binary search `d` between the smallest and largest bloom day.
4. **Feasibility of day `d`:** scan left to right and count consecutive bloomed flowers (those with `bloomDay[i] <= d`). Each time the run reaches `k`, make one bouquet and reset the run. Any unbloomed flower also resets the run. Day `d` works if the bouquet count reaches `m`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MinDays(int[] bloomDay, int m, int k) {
        int n = bloomDay.Length;
        if ((long)m * k > n) return -1;
        int lo = int.MaxValue, hi = 0;
        foreach (var d in bloomDay) {
            if (d < lo) lo = d;
            if (d > hi) hi = d;
        }
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            int count = 0, run = 0;
            foreach (var d in bloomDay) {
                if (d <= mid) {
                    run++;
                    if (run == k) { count++; run = 0; }
                } else run = 0;
                if (count >= m) break;
            }
            if (count >= m) hi = mid;
            else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }
}
```

