The answer is the first element plus every upward step between neighbours: `target[0] + sum(max(0, target[i] - target[i-1]))`.

Why this works: an operation that covers index `i` but not `i-1` must start exactly at `i`. Every unit by which `target[i]` exceeds `target[i-1]` therefore needs a new operation starting at `i`. Index 0 needs `target[0]` operations starting there. Wherever the value stays level or drops, the operations already running can simply continue or stop, so no new ones are needed.

Checking the examples:
- `[1,2,3,2,1]`: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3.
- `[3,1,1,2]`: 3 + 1 = 4.
- `[3,1,5,4,2]`: 3 + 4 = 7.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MinNumberOperations(int[] target) {
        long res = target[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < target.Length; i++) {
            if (target[i] > target[i - 1]) res += target[i] - target[i - 1];
        }
        return (int)res;
    }
}
```

