inference_trace:
Greedy approach: take the largest elements first.

1. Compute the total sum of `nums`.
2. Sort the array in descending order.
3. Add elements to the answer one at a time, keeping a running sum. Stop as soon as the running sum is strictly greater than `total - running sum` (the sum of the excluded elements).

Picking the largest values first reaches the threshold with the fewest elements, so the size is minimal. Among subsets of that size, the largest values also give the maximum total sum. The result is already in non-increasing order because the array was sorted that way.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution {
    public IList<int> MinSubsequence(int[] nums) {
        int total = nums.Sum();
        var sorted = nums.OrderByDescending(x => x).ToArray();
        var res = new List<int>();
        int s = 0;
        foreach (var x in sorted) {
            s += x;
            res.Add(x);
            if (s > total - s) break;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

