inference_trace:
Use a monotonic stack of indices and walk the array twice so the search can wrap around.

1. Fill the result array with `-1`.
2. Loop `i` from `0` to `2n - 1` and look at `nums[i % n]`.
3. While the stack is not empty and the value at the top index is smaller than `nums[i % n]`, pop that index. Set its answer to `nums[i % n]`.
4. During the first pass (`i < n`), push `i` onto the stack.

The stack always holds indices whose values are non-increasing and still need an answer. The second pass lets elements near the end find a greater value at the start of the array.

Each index is pushed at most once and popped at most once, so the time is O(n) and the extra space is O(n).

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int[] NextGreaterElements(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        int[] res = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) res[i] = -1;
        var stack = new Stack<int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
            int v = nums[i % n];
            while (stack.Count > 0 && nums[stack.Peek()] < v) {
                res[stack.Pop()] = v;
            }
            if (i < n) stack.Push(i);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

