Since n is at most 10^6, the answer is at most 1224444, which is the smallest balanced number with 7 digits. So brute force works: start at n+1 and test each number in turn.

The search gap is small. For n near 10^6, the scan runs from about 666667 up to 1224444, roughly 560k numbers. Each check costs about 7 digit operations, so the total work is tiny.

To test a number `x`:
1. Count how often each digit appears in `x`.
2. A digit 0 cannot appear at all, because it would need to occur 0 times.
3. For every digit `d` that appears, its count must equal `d`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int NextBeautifulNumber(int n) {
        for (int x = n + 1; ; x++) {
            if (Ok(x)) return x;
        }
    }
    private bool Ok(int x) {
        int[] c = new int[10];
        while (x > 0) { c[x % 10]++; x /= 10; }
        if (c[0] > 0) return false;
        for (int d = 1; d < 10; d++) {
            if (c[d] != 0 && c[d] != d) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

