inference_trace:
The idea: an ordering builds the same BST exactly when the root stays first, and within each subtree the relative order of its elements also builds that subtree. Elements of the left and right subtrees can be interleaved freely.

1. For a subarray with root `r = list[0]`, split the rest into:
   - `left`: elements smaller than `r`, kept in their original order.
   - `right`: elements larger than `r`, kept in their original order.
2. The number of valid orderings is:
   `ways(list) = C(L+R, L) * ways(left) * ways(right)`,
   where `L = |left|` and `R = |right|`. The binomial counts the ways to interleave the two subsequences.
3. The final answer is `ways(nums) - 1`, because the original ordering itself is excluded. The result is taken modulo `10^9 + 7`.

Implementation details:
- Precompute Pascal's triangle up to 1000 for the binomial coefficients.
- Recursion depth can reach 1000 when the input is sorted. That is fine on the default stack.
- Total work is O(n²), which is acceptable for n ≤ 1000.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    const long MOD = 1000000007L;
    long[,] C;

    public int NumOfWays(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        C = new long[n + 1, n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            C[i, 0] = 1;
            for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
                C[i, j] = (C[i - 1, j - 1] + C[i - 1, j]) % MOD;
            }
        }
        var list = new List<int>(nums);
        long res = Ways(list);
        return (int)((res - 1 + MOD) % MOD);
    }

    long Ways(List<int> list) {
        if (list.Count <= 2) return 1;
        int root = list[0];
        var left = new List<int>();
        var right = new List<int>();
        for (int i = 1; i < list.Count; i++) {
            if (list[i] < root) left.Add(list[i]);
            else right.Add(list[i]);
        }
        long l = Ways(left);
        long r = Ways(right);
        return C[left.Count + right.Count, left.Count] * l % MOD * r % MOD;
    }
}
```

