inference_trace:
For each array, count how often each square appears. Then look at every pair `(j, k)` with `j < k` in the other array and add the count of squares equal to their product.

- Each array has at most 1000 elements, so there are about 500k pairs per direction. That is fast enough.
- Values go up to 10^5, so squares and products reach 10^10. Both must be computed as `long`.
- The total number of triplets is at most about 1000 × 500k = 5·10^8 per type. The combined total therefore fits in an `int`, but it is accumulated as `long` anyway for safety.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int NumTriplets(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
        return (int)(Count(nums1, nums2) + Count(nums2, nums1));
    }

    private long Count(int[] a, int[] b) {
        var sq = new Dictionary<long, int>();
        foreach (var x in a) {
            long s = (long)x * x;
            sq.TryGetValue(s, out int c);
            sq[s] = c + 1;
        }
        long res = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < b.Length; j++) {
            for (int k = j + 1; k < b.Length; k++) {
                long p = (long)b[j] * b[k];
                if (sq.TryGetValue(p, out int c)) res += c;
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

