Approach: bitmask DP over which elements have been used.

1. Let `sum` be the total of `nums`. If `sum % k != 0`, return false. Otherwise each subset must sum to `target = sum / k`.
2. Sort `nums`. If the largest element exceeds `target`, return false.
3. Define `dp[mask]` as the running sum of the current (partially filled) bucket after using exactly the elements in `mask`, or -1 if that mask is unreachable. Start with `dp[0] = 0`.
4. For each reachable mask, try each unused element `i`:
   - If `dp[mask] + nums[i] > target`, stop trying further `i`. This break is safe because the array is sorted, so all later elements are at least as large.
   - Otherwise set `dp[mask | 1<<i] = (dp[mask] + nums[i]) % target`. The modulo starts a new bucket whenever the current one is exactly filled.
5. The answer is true if the full mask is reachable, i.e. `dp[(1<<n) - 1] == 0`.

With `n <= 16` there are at most 65,536 masks, and each tries at most 16 elements, so the work is about 1M steps.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool CanPartitionKSubsets(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        int sum = 0;
        foreach (var x in nums) sum += x;
        if (sum % k != 0) return false;
        int target = sum / k;
        Array.Sort(nums);
        if (nums[n - 1] > target) return false;
        int full = 1 << n;
        int[] dp = new int[full];
        for (int i = 0; i < full; i++) dp[i] = -1;
        dp[0] = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] == -1) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << i)) != 0) continue;
                int s = dp[mask] + nums[i];
                if (s > target) break;
                int nm = mask | (1 << i);
                if (dp[nm] == -1) dp[nm] = s % target;
            }
        }
        return dp[full - 1] == 0;
    }
}
```

inference_trace:
The problem reduces to a known fact: picking `n` slices from the circle of `3n` is possible exactly when no two chosen slices are adjacent (including the wrap-around between the first and last slice). So we want the maximum sum of `n` non-adjacent elements in a circular array.

To handle the circle, solve the linear version twice and take the better result:
- once on the range `[0, m-2]` (excluding the last slice),
- once on the range `[1, m-1]` (excluding the first slice).

For each linear range, use a DP:
- `dp[i][j]` is the best sum using the first `i` elements of the range while picking exactly `j` of them.
- `dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i-2][j-1] + a[i])`.
- Unreachable states are set to negative infinity.

Edge case: when `m == 3`, `n == 1`, each range has 2 elements, so the answer is the max of those. The general DP already handles this.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MaxSizeSlices(int[] slices) {
        int m = slices.Length;
        int n = m / 3;
        return System.Math.Max(Solve(slices, 0, m - 2, n), Solve(slices, 1, m - 1, n));
    }
    private int Solve(int[] a, int lo, int hi, int n) {
        int len = hi - lo + 1;
        const int NEG = int.MinValue / 4;
        int[,] dp = new int[len + 1, n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= len; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
                dp[i, j] = NEG;
        for (int i = 1; i <= len; i++) {
            int v = a[lo + i - 1];
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                int best = dp[i - 1, j];
                int prev = i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2, j - 1] : (j - 1 == 0 ? 0 : NEG);
                if (prev > NEG) best = System.Math.Max(best, prev + v);
                dp[i, j] = best;
            }
        }
        return dp[len, n];
    }
}
```

