inference_trace:
For each request, use a union-find over the people.

1. Find the roots of the two people, `pu` and `pv`.
2. If `pu == pv`, they are already in the same friend group, so the request succeeds.
3. Otherwise, check every restriction `[x, y]`. Let `rx` and `ry` be the roots of `x` and `y`. If `{rx, ry}` equals `{pu, pv}`, merging would make a restricted pair friends, so the request fails.
4. If no restriction matches, merge the two groups and the request succeeds.

Each request checks all restrictions once, so the total work is about 1000 × 1000 restriction checks. This is fast enough with path compression.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    int[] p;
    int Find(int x) {
        while (p[x] != x) { p[x] = p[p[x]]; x = p[x]; }
        return x;
    }
    public bool[] FriendRequests(int n, int[][] restrictions, int[][] requests) {
        p = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) p[i] = i;
        bool[] res = new bool[requests.Length];
        for (int j = 0; j < requests.Length; j++) {
            int pu = Find(requests[j][0]), pv = Find(requests[j][1]);
            if (pu == pv) { res[j] = true; continue; }
            bool ok = true;
            foreach (var r in restrictions) {
                int rx = Find(r[0]), ry = Find(r[1]);
                if ((rx == pu && ry == pv) || (rx == pv && ry == pu)) { ok = false; break; }
            }
            if (ok) { p[pu] = pv; }
            res[j] = ok;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

