inference_trace:
Use bottom-up dynamic programming. Let `dp[i][j]` be true when the suffix `s[i..]` matches the suffix `p[j..]`.

**Base case:** `dp[m][n] = true`, since an empty string matches an empty pattern.

**Transition:** fill the table backwards, for `i` from `m` down to `0` and `j` from `n - 1` down to `0`.

- `first` is true when `i < m` and `p[j]` matches `s[i]`, meaning `p[j] == s[i]` or `p[j] == '.'`.
- If the next pattern character `p[j+1]` is `'*'`, there are two choices:
  - Use zero copies of `p[j]`: take `dp[i][j+2]`.
  - Use one more copy: take `first && dp[i+1][j]`.
- Otherwise, the result is `first && dp[i+1][j+1]`.

The answer is `dp[0][0]`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool IsMatch(string s, string p) {
        int m = s.Length, n = p.Length;
        bool[,] dp = new bool[m + 1, n + 1];
        dp[m, n] = true;
        for (int i = m; i >= 0; i--) {
            for (int j = n - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
                bool first = i < m && (p[j] == s[i] || p[j] == '.');
                if (j + 1 < n && p[j + 1] == '*') {
                    dp[i, j] = dp[i, j + 2] || (first && dp[i + 1, j]);
                } else {
                    dp[i, j] = first && dp[i + 1, j + 1];
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[0, 0];
    }
}
```

