Model the pairs as a graph: each number is a node and each pair is an edge. The original array is a simple path through this graph.

1. Build an adjacency list mapping each value to its neighbors.
2. The endpoints of the array have exactly one neighbor. Pick one of them as `res[0]`.
3. Walk the path: `res[1]` is the only neighbor of `res[0]`. For each later position, take the neighbor of the current element that is not the previous element.

Each node has at most two neighbors, so every step is constant time and the whole solution is O(n).

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int[] RestoreArray(int[][] adjacentPairs) {
        var adj = new Dictionary<int, List<int>>();
        foreach (var p in adjacentPairs) {
            if (!adj.TryGetValue(p[0], out var a)) { a = new List<int>(2); adj[p[0]] = a; }
            if (!adj.TryGetValue(p[1], out var b)) { b = new List<int>(2); adj[p[1]] = b; }
            a.Add(p[1]);
            b.Add(p[0]);
        }
        int n = adjacentPairs.Length + 1;
        int[] res = new int[n];
        foreach (var kv in adj) {
            if (kv.Value.Count == 1) { res[0] = kv.Key; break; }
        }
        res[1] = adj[res[0]][0];
        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            var l = adj[res[i - 1]];
            res[i] = l[0] == res[i - 2] ? l[1] : l[0];
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

