inference_trace:
Sort the products with ordinal comparison, then keep a shrinking window `[lo, hi]` of products that still match the prefix typed so far.

1. After typing character `i` (`c = searchWord[i]`):
   - Move `lo` forward while the product at `lo` is too short (`length <= i`) or has a different character at position `i`.
   - Move `hi` backward under the same condition.
2. Because the list is sorted and every product in the window already shares the previous prefix, the products whose character `i` equals `c` form a contiguous block. So narrowing from both ends is correct.
3. Take up to three products starting at `lo`. If the window is empty, add an empty list for this step.

Each pointer only moves inward, so after sorting the scan costs about `O(n + m)`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public IList<IList<string>> SuggestedProducts(string[] products, string searchWord) {
        Array.Sort(products, StringComparer.Ordinal);
        var res = new List<IList<string>>();
        int lo = 0, hi = products.Length - 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < searchWord.Length; i++) {
            char c = searchWord[i];
            while (lo <= hi && (products[lo].Length <= i || products[lo][i] != c)) lo++;
            while (lo <= hi && (products[hi].Length <= i || products[hi][i] != c)) hi--;
            var list = new List<string>();
            for (int k = lo; k <= hi && k < lo + 3; k++) list.Add(products[k]);
            res.Add(list);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

