Treat the grid as one flattened array of length `total = m * n`. Each shift moves every element one position forward in that array, with wraparound. So after `k` shifts, the element at flat index `i` ends up at index `(i + k) % total`.

Steps:
1. Reduce `k` to `k % total`, since shifting `total` times returns the original grid.
2. Allocate an `m x n` result.
3. For each source index `i`, compute its destination `d = (i + k) % total`.
4. Write `grid[i / n][i % n]` into `res[d / n][d % n]`.

This runs in O(m·n) time and uses O(m·n) extra space for the result.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public IList<IList<int>> ShiftGrid(int[][] grid, int k) {
        int m = grid.Length, n = grid[0].Length;
        int total = m * n;
        k %= total;
        int[][] res = new int[m][];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) res[i] = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < total; i++) {
            int d = (i + k) % total;
            res[d / n][d % n] = grid[i / n][i % n];
        }
        var result = new List<IList<int>>();
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) result.Add(new List<int>(res[i]));
        return result;
    }
}
```

