Approach: simulate the spiral walk directly, recording only the cells that land inside the grid.

1. Record the start cell first.
2. Walk in the direction order east, south, west, north.
3. The segment lengths follow the pattern 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, ... — the length grows by one after every two turns.
4. After each single step, if the current cell is inside the grid, add it to the result.
5. Stop as soon as all `rows * cols` cells have been recorded.

The spiral grows at most until it covers the grid, so the number of steps is O(max(rows, cols)²).

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int[][] SpiralMatrixIII(int rows, int cols, int rStart, int cStart) {
        int total = rows * cols;
        int[][] res = new int[total][];
        int idx = 0;
        res[idx++] = new int[]{rStart, cStart};
        int[] dr = {0, 1, 0, -1};
        int[] dc = {1, 0, -1, 0};
        int r = rStart, c = cStart;
        int len = 1, d = 0;
        while (idx < total) {
            for (int t = 0; t < 2 && idx < total; t++) {
                for (int s = 0; s < len; s++) {
                    r += dr[d];
                    c += dc[d];
                    if (r >= 0 && r < rows && c >= 0 && c < cols) {
                        res[idx++] = new int[]{r, c};
                        if (idx == total) break;
                    }
                }
                d = (d + 1) % 4;
            }
            len++;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

