inference_trace:
Binary search on the answer, the largest allowed subarray sum.

1. The answer lies between `lo = max(nums)` and `hi = sum(nums)`.
2. For a candidate value `mid`, scan the array greedily. Start a new subarray whenever adding the next element would push the running sum above `mid`. Count how many subarrays this needs.
3. If the count is at most `k`, `mid` is feasible. Fewer pieces are fine, because any piece can be split further without raising the maximum, as long as there are enough elements. Since `k <= n`, this always holds. So set `hi = mid`.
4. Otherwise set `lo = mid + 1`.
5. When the search ends, `lo` is the minimized largest sum.

The total sum is at most 1000 × 10^6 = 10^9, which fits in an `int`, but `long` is used for safety.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int SplitArray(int[] nums, int k) {
        long lo = 0, hi = 0;
        foreach (var x in nums) { lo = System.Math.Max(lo, x); hi += x; }
        while (lo < hi) {
            long mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            int cnt = 1; long cur = 0;
            foreach (var x in nums) {
                if (cur + x > mid) { cnt++; cur = x; }
                else cur += x;
            }
            if (cnt <= k) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        return (int)lo;
    }
}
```

