inference_trace:
Use a bitmask DP over the positions of `target` (it has at most 15 characters, so at most 2^15 masks).

- Let `dp[mask]` be the minimum number of stickers needed to cover the target positions whose bits are set in `mask`. Start with `dp[0] = 0`.
- Process masks in increasing order. Skip any mask with no finite `dp` value.
- For each reachable mask, apply each sticker greedily:
  - Copy the sticker's letter counts.
  - For each target position `i` not yet in the mask, if the sticker still has that letter, set bit `i` and use up one count.
- Update `dp[newMask] = min(dp[newMask], dp[mask] + 1)`.

Applying a sticker only ever adds bits, so `newMask > mask` whenever something changes. That means processing masks in increasing order is valid.

**Pruning.** Only try stickers that contain the first unset character of the target. Some sticker must eventually cover that position, so the order of stickers doesn't matter for this choice.

**Cost.** 32768 masks × 50 stickers × 15 positions is about 24M operations, which is fine.

**Answer.** Return `dp[full]` if it is finite, otherwise -1.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MinStickers(string[] stickers, string target) {
        int n = target.Length;
        int full = (1 << n) - 1;
        int m = stickers.Length;
        int[][] cnt = new int[m][];
        for (int s = 0; s < m; s++) {
            cnt[s] = new int[26];
            foreach (char c in stickers[s]) cnt[s][c - 'a']++;
        }
        int[] dp = new int[1 << n];
        for (int i = 0; i <= full; i++) dp[i] = int.MaxValue;
        dp[0] = 0;
        int[] tmp = new int[26];
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] == int.MaxValue) continue;
            int first = -1;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if ((mask & (1 << i)) == 0) { first = i; break; }
            int fc = target[first] - 'a';
            for (int s = 0; s < m; s++) {
                if (cnt[s][fc] == 0) continue;
                Array.Copy(cnt[s], tmp, 26);
                int nm = mask;
                for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                    if ((nm & (1 << i)) != 0) continue;
                    int c = target[i] - 'a';
                    if (tmp[c] > 0) { tmp[c]--; nm |= 1 << i; }
                }
                if (dp[mask] + 1 < dp[nm]) dp[nm] = dp[mask] + 1;
            }
        }
        return dp[full] == int.MaxValue ? -1 : dp[full];
    }
}
```

