inference_trace:
Greedy approach: build the string one character at a time.

At each step, decide whether to write an 'a' or a 'b':
- If the last two characters are the same letter, we must write the other letter. Otherwise we would create "aaa" or "bbb".
- Otherwise, write the letter that has more remaining copies. This keeps the counts balanced so we never get forced into a triple later.

After writing a letter, decrement its remaining count. Repeat until both counts reach zero. The problem guarantees a valid string exists, so this greedy choice always succeeds.

```csharp
using System.Text;

public class Solution {
    public string StrWithout3a3b(int a, int b) {
        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        while (a > 0 || b > 0) {
            int n = sb.Length;
            bool writeA;
            if (n >= 2 && sb[n - 1] == sb[n - 2]) {
                writeA = sb[n - 1] == 'b';
            } else {
                writeA = a >= b;
            }
            if (writeA) {
                sb.Append('a');
                a--;
            } else {
                sb.Append('b');
                b--;
            }
        }
        return sb.ToString();
    }
}
```

