Values only range over 1..50, so instead of checking every ancestor we track, for each value, its deepest occurrence on the current root-to-node path.

**Approach**

1. Precompute `cop[a][b] = (gcd(a, b) == 1)` for all `a, b` in 1..50.
2. Run an iterative DFS from node 0, since n can be 10^5 and recursion could overflow the stack.
3. During the DFS, keep for each value `v` a stack of `(node, depth)` pairs for the ancestors on the current path that have value `v`.
4. When entering node `u`:
   - For each value `v` in 1..50 with `cop[nums[u]][v]` true and a non-empty stack, look at the top of that stack.
   - Among those tops, pick the node with the greatest depth. That node is `ans[u]`; if none exists, `ans[u] = -1`.
   - Then push `(u, depth)` onto the stack for `nums[u]`.
5. When leaving node `u`, pop it from the stack for `nums[u]`.

**Implementation details**

- The stacks are fixed-size arrays per value. A depth-indexed array per value would cost 51 × n memory, which is too much. Instead, use a `List<int>` per value storing node ids, plus a global `depth[]` array to look up each node's depth.
- The iterative DFS uses an explicit stack of nodes and an iterator index into each node's adjacency list.
- Adjacency is built in CSR form: count degrees, compute prefix offsets, then fill the neighbor array.

**Complexity:** O(50 · n) time and O(n) extra memory.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int[] GetCoprimes(int[] nums, int[][] edges) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        bool[,] cop = new bool[51, 51];
        for (int a = 1; a <= 50; a++)
            for (int b = 1; b <= 50; b++) {
                int x = a, y = b;
                while (y != 0) { int t = x % y; x = y; y = t; }
                cop[a, b] = x == 1;
            }
        int[] deg = new int[n + 1];
        foreach (var e in edges) { deg[e[0]]++; deg[e[1]]++; }
        int[] start = new int[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) start[i + 1] = start[i] + deg[i];
        int[] adj = new int[2 * (n - 1) + 1];
        int[] fill = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) fill[i] = start[i];
        foreach (var e in edges) { adj[fill[e[0]]++] = e[1]; adj[fill[e[1]]++] = e[0]; }

        var stacks = new List<int>[51];
        for (int v = 0; v <= 50; v++) stacks[v] = new List<int>();
        int[] depth = new int[n];
        int[] parent = new int[n];
        int[] it = new int[n];
        int[] ans = new int[n];
        int[] st = new int[n];
        int sp = 0;

        // enter node 0
        st[sp++] = 0; parent[0] = -1; depth[0] = 0; it[0] = start[0];
        ans[0] = -1;
        stacks[nums[0]].Add(0);

        while (sp > 0) {
            int u = st[sp - 1];
            if (it[u] < start[u + 1]) {
                int w = adj[it[u]++];
                if (w == parent[u]) continue;
                parent[w] = u; depth[w] = depth[u] + 1; it[w] = start[w];
                int best = -1, bd = -1;
                int val = nums[w];
                for (int v = 1; v <= 50; v++) {
                    if (!cop[val, v]) continue;
                    var s = stacks[v];
                    if (s.Count == 0) continue;
                    int nd = s[s.Count - 1];
                    if (depth[nd] > bd) { bd = depth[nd]; best = nd; }
                }
                ans[w] = best;
                stacks[val].Add(w);
                st[sp++] = w;
            } else {
                var s = stacks[nums[u]];
                s.RemoveAt(s.Count - 1);
                sp--;
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

