inference_trace:
Approach:

1. **Count parents.** For every child index that is not -1, increase its in-degree. If any node ends up with two parents, return false.
2. **Find the root.** Exactly one node must have in-degree 0. If there are zero such nodes or more than one, return false.
3. **Check reachability.** Run a BFS from the root using a visited array. If we ever reach a node twice, the structure has a cycle or shared child, so return false.
4. **Confirm all nodes are used.** The answer is true only if the BFS visits exactly `n` nodes.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool ValidateBinaryTreeNodes(int n, int[] leftChild, int[] rightChild) {
        int[] indeg = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (leftChild[i] != -1 && ++indeg[leftChild[i]] > 1) return false;
            if (rightChild[i] != -1 && ++indeg[rightChild[i]] > 1) return false;
        }
        int root = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (indeg[i] == 0) {
                if (root != -1) return false;
                root = i;
            }
        }
        if (root == -1) return false;
        bool[] visited = new bool[n];
        var q = new System.Collections.Generic.Queue<int>();
        q.Enqueue(root);
        visited[root] = true;
        int count = 0;
        while (q.Count > 0) {
            int u = q.Dequeue();
            count++;
            foreach (int c in new[] { leftChild[u], rightChild[u] }) {
                if (c == -1) continue;
                if (visited[c]) return false;
                visited[c] = true;
                q.Enqueue(c);
            }
        }
        return count == n;
    }
}
```

