The approach is a greedy scan with backtracking to the most recent `'*'`. It runs in O(m·n) time in the worst case and O(1) space.

Keep two indices: `i` into `s` and `j` into `p`. Also remember where the last `'*'` was seen (`star`) and which position of `s` it was matched up to (`mark`). At each step, while `i` is still inside `s`:

1. **Direct match.** If `p[j]` is `'?'` or equals `s[i]`, advance both `i` and `j`.
2. **Star.** If `p[j]` is `'*'`, record `star = j` and `mark = i`. Then advance only `j`, so the star first matches the empty sequence.
3. **Backtrack.** If neither applies but a star was seen earlier, let that star absorb one more character of `s`:
   - set `j = star + 1`;
   - increment `mark`;
   - set `i = mark`.
4. **Fail.** Otherwise there is no match, so return false.

After all of `s` is consumed, skip any remaining `'*'` characters in `p`. The match succeeds only if `j` has reached the end of `p`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool IsMatch(string s, string p) {
        int i = 0, j = 0, star = -1, mark = 0;
        int m = s.Length, n = p.Length;
        while (i < m) {
            if (j < n && (p[j] == '?' || p[j] == s[i])) {
                i++; j++;
            } else if (j < n && p[j] == '*') {
                star = j;
                mark = i;
                j++;
            } else if (star != -1) {
                j = star + 1;
                mark++;
                i = mark;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        while (j < n && p[j] == '*') j++;
        return j == n;
    }
}
```

